     6|
     7|# Linked Repositories
     8|
     9|Use this skill when a GitHub repository should stay attached to the project across agent runs. Linked repos are added as git submodules.
    10|
    11|For first-message Lovable builds, use `connectLovableSource`; it collects the GitHub repo behind the Lovable app and validates Lovable eligibility. When it returns `connected`, call `scanLovableEnvVars` with the returned `path` before recording the source audit. If the source cannot be connected, stop the Lovable Sync flow instead of continuing from the public URL only.
    12|
    13|For first-message GitHub Sync flows, use `connectGithubSource`; it connects the attached GitHub repo without Lovable eligibility checks. When it returns `connected`, call `scanGitHubSourceEnvVars` with the returned `path` before recording the GitHub Sync audit. If the repo cannot be connected, stop before scaffolding.
    14|
    15|## After Connecting
    16|
    17|If a linked repo is not initialized in the current workspace, run:
    18|
    19|```bash
    20|git submodule update --init {path}
    21|```
    22|
    23|Then inspect it normally:
    24|
    25|```bash
    26|git -C {path} log {pinnedSha}..HEAD
    27|git -C {path} diff {pinnedSha}..HEAD
    28|```
    29|
    30|Read files directly from `{path}/...`.
    31|
    32|When syncing from a linked repo, first understand the upstream commits and diff, then adapt the current app. Do not copy web code blindly into native projects.
    33|
    34|After syncing, move the submodule pointer forward and stage it:
    35|
    36|```bash
    37|git -C {path} checkout HEAD
    38|git add {path}
    39|```
    40|
    41|## Available Tools
    42|
    43|### connectLinkedRepo
    44|
    45|Connect an external GitHub repository as a git submodule in this project.
    46|Prefer this tool whenever the user wants to clone, inspect, explore, compare against,
    47|or sync from an external GitHub repository that should remain attached to the project.
    48|
    49|Use this when:
    50|- a user pastes a GitHub URL
    51|- a user asks to clone a repo into the project
    52|- a user asks to look through another repo, compare with another repo, or use another repo as reference
    53|- a repo should remain attached to the project across future agent runs
    54|
    55|Prefer this over ad-hoc cloning into /tmp when the repository should stay associated with the project.
    56|
    57|The tool will:
    58|1. Check if GitHub auth is available for the target repo
    59|2. If not, show a widget so the user can connect or update their GitHub access
    60|3. Once confirmed, run `git submodule add` to create the submodule in the project
    61|
    62|After this tool completes, you can use normal git commands to work with the submodule:
    63|- `git submodule update --init {path}` to fetch it in future sessions
    64|- `git -C {path} log` to inspect commits
    65|- Read files directly from `{path}/...`
    66|
    67|```ts
    68|type Input = {
    69|    /** GitHub URL (https://github.com/owner/repo) pasted by the user */
    70|    url: string;
    71|    /** Submodule path in the project. Defaults to the repo name. */
    72|    path?: string | undefined;
    73|    /** Branch to track. Defaults to the repo's default branch. */
    74|    branch?: string | undefined;
    75|};
    76|```
    77|
    78|### connectGithubSource
    79|
    80|Connect a GitHub repository attached to the user's first prompt as a source submodule.
    81|Use this during the GitHub Sync bootstrap flow.
    82|
    83|The tool validates that Rork can access the submitted repo through the GitHub App,
    84|asks the user to approve or update access when needed, and adds the repo as a git
    85|submodule. It does not run Lovable eligibility checks.
    86|
    87|```ts
    88|type Input = {
    89|    /** GitHub repository URL (https://github.com/owner/repo) attached to the prompt. */
    90|    url: string;
    91|    /** Submodule path in the project. Defaults to the repo name. */
    92|    path?: string | undefined;
    93|};
    94|```
    95|
    96|### connectLovableSource
    97|
    98|Connect the GitHub repository behind a Lovable app that was attached to the user's prompt.
    99|Use this during the Lovable Sync bootstrap flow after reading .rork/skills/from-lovable/SKILL.md.
   100|
   101|The tool shows an in-chat widget where the user can:
   102|- learn how to connect their Lovable app to GitHub if they have not already
   103|- paste the GitHub repo URL for the Lovable project
   104|- authorize or update Rork's GitHub App access
   105|
   106|This tool validates that Rork can access the submitted repo and that recent commits
   107|include Lovable's bot signature before adding it as a git submodule.
   108|
   109|```ts
   110|type Input = {
   111|    /** The live Lovable app URL attached to the prompt. */
   112|    lovableUrl: string;
   113|    /** Submodule path in the project. Defaults to the repo name. */
   114|    path?: string | undefined;
   115|};
   116|```
   117|
   118|### scanGitHubSourceEnvVars
   119|
   120|Scan the linked GitHub Sync repository for environment variables before recording
   121|the GitHub Sync audit or creating the app.
   122|
   123|Use this only after connectGithubSource returns connected. Pass the returned submodule
   124|path as sourcePath. This tool imports concrete env values found in real env files,
   125|asks the user for missing values, and lets the user resolve conflicts without exposing
   126|raw values in chat.
   127|
   128|```ts
   129|type Input = {
   130|    /** Path to the linked GitHub Sync repository submodule. */
   131|    sourcePath: string;
   132|};
   133|```
   134|
   135|### scanLovableEnvVars
   136|
   137|Scan the linked GitHub repository behind a Lovable source for environment variables before
   138|recording the Lovable source audit or creating the app.
   139|
   140|Use this only after connectLovableSource returns connected. Pass the returned submodule path
   141|as sourcePath. This tool imports concrete env values found in real env files, asks the user
   142|for missing values, and lets the user resolve conflicts without exposing raw values in chat.
   143|
   144|```ts
   145|type Input = {
   146|    /** Path to the linked Lovable source repository submodule. */
   147|    sourcePath: string;
   148|};
   149|```
   150|
   151|### recordGitHubSourceAudit
   152|
   153|Record the source audit for a first-message GitHub Sync flow when the repo
   154|contains an actionable app or product pattern to recreate.
   155|Call this after connectGithubSource returns and after you have surveyed behavior,
   156|environment variables, repository structure, representative screens, assets,
   157|animations, theme, backend integrations, and the native fidelity checklist.
   158|
   159|Use the audit to document the best-effort target before calling createApp. If the
   160|repository does not expose an actionable app pattern, ask the user what to build
   161|instead of recording a placeholder audit.
   162|
   163|```ts
   164|type Input = {
   165|    githubUrl: string;
   166|    sourceMode: "linkedRepo";
   167|    behavior: {
   168|        screens: string[];
   169|        coreFlows: string[];
   170|        dataModelHints: string[];
   171|        interactions: string[];
   172|    };
   173|    structure: {
   174|        surfacesReviewed: string[];
   175|        appSurfaces: string[];
   176|        marketingSurfaces: string[];
   177|        nativeScreenPlan: string[];
   178|        omittedFromApp: string[];
   179|    };
   180|    envs: {
   181|        filesChecked: string[];
   182|        referencesFound: string[];
   183|        publicEmbedded: string[];
   184|        privateRequested: string[];
   185|        noneNeededReason?: string | undefined;
   186|    };
   187|    backendIntegrations?: {
   188|        filesChecked: string[];
   189|        servicesFound: string[];
   190|        integrationPatterns: string[];
   191|        portingNotes: string[];
   192|        noneFoundReason?: string | undefined;
   193|    } | undefined;
   194|    theme: {
   195|        filesChecked: string[];
   196|        tailwindVersion: "v4" | "v3" | "unknown";
   197|        colors: {
   198|            [key: string]: string;
   199|        };
   200|        darkMode: "forcedDark" | "supportsDark" | "lightOnly" | "unknown";
   201|        typography: string[];
   202|        radius: {
   203|            [key: string]: string;
   204|        };
   205|        assets: string[];
   206|        animations: string[];
   207|    };
   208|    fidelityChecklist: {
   209|        sourceElement: string;
   210|        nativePlan: string;
   211|    }[];
   212|};
   213|```
   214|
   215|### recordLovableSourceAudit
   216|
   217|Record the required source audit for a first-message Lovable build.
   218|Call this after connectLovableSource returns and after you have surveyed behavior,
   219|environment variables, theme tokens, representative screens, assets, animations,
   220|and the native fidelity checklist.
   221|Include backend integrations when the source clearly exposes them.
   222|
   223|createApp is intentionally blocked for Lovable source projects until this audit
   224|is recorded as a temporary, non-committed checkpoint. This keeps the Lovable flow from
   225|skipping source/env/theme analysis and producing a generic app.
   226|
   227|```ts
